      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">
        LIC Claim When the Policyholder Is Missing: The 7-Year Rule, Court Order &amp; Documents (2026)
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        Short answer: <strong>yes, the claim can be paid — but not before seven years have passed since the person was last heard of, and not without a court order.</strong> There is no death certificate when there is no body, so the law supplies a substitute: under Sections 107–108 of the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 (now re-enacted in the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023), a person who has not been heard of for seven years by those who would naturally have heard of them is presumed dead. LIC's own Insurance Manual, Rule 14, says the same thing — the life assured is presumed dead after seven years, or on production of a decree from a court of law. The part that destroys most claims is not the seven-year wait: it is the premiums. <strong>The policy must be kept in force by paying premiums regularly through all seven years.</strong> The Supreme Court said so plainly in <em>LIC v Anuradha</em> (2004) — a policy allowed to lapse pays only the paid-up value, however long the person has been missing. Everything below was re-checked on 23 November 2026 against the Supreme Court judgment, LIC's claim forms page, and IRDAI's claim-settlement framework.
      </p>
    </div>

